<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ravi Shankar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/2012">2012</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> Shankar was an Indian sitar virtuoso and composer who trained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allauddin-khan">Allauddin Khan</a> at Maihar. From the 1950s he brought Hindustani classical music to Western audiences through tours, recordings and his work with the Beatles' George Harrison and the violinist Yehudi Menuhin. He received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s highest civilian honour, the Bharat Ratna, in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ravi-shankar.jpg" alt="Ravi Shankar" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markgoff2972 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ravi_Shankar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
